+//! The read side of the store, as an interface another program consumes.
+//!
+//! `lakeletd` is a process a supervisor starts; `lakeletq` is a tool a *script* runs, and a script
+//! is a customer with no patience and no imagination: it reads `$?`, it reads stdout, it reads
+//! stderr into a log, and it cannot answer a question. This module is the grammar that customer
+//! gets, and it is `jobq/3.15-cli-contract`'s contract re-stood rather than a fifth idea about
+//! command lines:
+//!
+//! * **stdout is the answer, stderr is the diagnosis.** A command that answered prints its answer
+//!   on stdout and nothing on stderr; a command that failed prints *nothing* on stdout and one
+//!   sentence on stderr. A script that pipes stdout into a parser must never be handed half an
+//!   answer plus a warning, so the two streams are decided here, as one value ([`Outcome`]),
+//!   instead of at five call sites that each remember the rule.
+//! * **one exit code per failure class.** `0` answered, `2` the call was wrong, `3` the thing
+//!   asked about is not there, `4` the store refused, `1` this deployment is broken. Every caller
+//!   mistake is answered *before* a store is opened, so a `2` is the script's problem wherever the
+//!   deployment happens to be.
+//! * **`--json` is a format, not a second command.** It changes what the answer looks like and
+//!   nothing else: the code and the diagnosis are identical with and without it, and an empty
+//!   answer is `[]` rather than nothing at all.
+//! * **nothing prompts.** Every input arrives as a flag, a call that is missing one names what is
+//!   missing, and a run that was not asked to read standard input never touches it — a tool that
+//!   blocked on a terminal would be a tool that hangs the pipeline it was meant to feed.
+//!
+//! # What this tool reads, and what it refuses to create
+//!
+//! `query` opens a store directory with
+//! [`Segments::open`](crate::engine::compaction::Segments::open), takes a
+//! [`snapshot`](crate::engine::compaction::Segments::snapshot), reads the records it names, hands
+//! them to [`downsample`](crate::engine::query::downsample::downsample) and writes the answer.
+//! Three things about that are decisions rather than plumbing:
+//!
+//! * **a store that is not there is not a store with nothing in it.** `Segments::open` *creates*
+//!   the directory it is given, which is right for a daemon and wrong for a read: a typo in
+//!   `--store` would leave an empty store behind and answer `0` with no lines, so `query` checks
+//!   first — no directory at that path is `3`, the thing asked about is not there;
+//! * **a path that is not a directory is a broken deployment, not a missing store.** A file where
+//!   the store should be is `1`: nothing about the *call* is wrong and nothing can be answered
+//!   either, and the difference between those two is the difference between the script's problem
+//!   and the operator's;
+//! * **the store's floor is an answer, not an error.** A store that has forgotten up to a
+//!   millisecond (`lakelet/4.9-retention`) answers with the records it still keeps, so an answer
+//!   may begin later than `--from` asked. That is `0` with fewer buckets, and
+//!   `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` is where a query *refuses* a range the store cannot answer
+//!   rather than answering what is left of it.
+//!
+//! # The two shapes
+//!
+//! ```text
+//! lakeletq query --store ./s --from 1700000000000 --to 1700003600000 --window 1h --agg mean
+//!   →  1700000000000 41 12.5
+//!      1700003600000 37 12.7
+//! lakeletq query … --json
+//!   →  [{"start":1700000000000,"count":41,"value":12.5},{"start":1700003600000,"count":37,"value":12.7}]
+//! ```
+//!
+//! One line per bucket, oldest window first, fields in the order `start count value` and separated
+//! by single spaces; a window that held no records is not a line (`lakelet/4.10-downsampling`'s
+//! "absent, not zero"). `--json` prints one array of objects with those three fields in that
+//! order, and `[]` when the range held nothing — a script that has to special-case "no output" is
+//! a script that gets it wrong once and then stops trusting the tool.
+//!
+//! # What the course gives you, and what is yours
+//!
+//! [`USAGE`] is the grammar — it is the interface a script reads and the contract this module
+//! keeps — and [`Outcome`] is the shape every path answers with. The parser, the two renderers and
+//! the constructors that put them together are yours: "stdout is the answer, stderr is the
+//! diagnosis" is what this step is about, and an [`Outcome`] that already knew the rule would be
+//! the answer.
+
+/// The usage text: what the tool is, what it reads, and the codes it leaves behind.
+pub const USAGE: &str = "\
+usage: lakeletq [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>
+
+COMMANDS:
+    query                   answer a question about a store
+    help                    print this help
+
+OPTIONS:
+    --json                  print the answer as one JSON value on stdout
+    --help, -h              print this help and exit
+    --version               print the version and exit
+
+QUERY OPTIONS:
+    query --store <DIR> --from <MS> --to <MS> --window <DURATION> --agg <NAME> [--series <SERIES>]
+          --store <DIR>     the store directory to read
+          --from <MS>       first millisecond of the range, since the Unix epoch (inclusive)
+          --to <MS>         first millisecond after it (exclusive)
+          --window <D>      how wide one bucket is: a number and a unit — 1500ms, 90s, 5m, 1h, 2d
+          --agg <NAME>      count, sum, mean, min or max
+          --series <SERIES> only this series (the default is every series, which is rarely what a
+                            mean wants)
+
+The answer is one line per bucket on stdout, `start count value`, oldest window first; a window
+that held no records is not a line. With --json it is one array of {\"start\":…,\"count\":…,\"value\":…},
+and [] when the range held nothing.
+
+Exit codes: 0 the command answered, 2 the call was wrong, 3 the thing asked about is not there,
+4 the store refused, 1 this deployment is broken.
+";
+
+/// Most buckets one query will answer.
+///
+/// A range and a window are two numbers a caller types, and their ratio is a number nobody types:
+/// `--window 1ms` over a year of range is thirty billion windows, which is a typo rather than a
+/// question. The count is arithmetic the parser can do before a store is opened, so it is a caller
+/// error (`2`) naming both numbers — and the number is high enough that it is never a page size: a
+/// million one-hour buckets is a hundred and fourteen years.
+pub const MAX_WINDOWS: u64 = 1_000_000;
+
+/// What the process would have written, and the code it would have ended with.
+///
+/// One value rather than three writes, and that is the shape of the contract: "stdout is the
+/// answer, stderr is the diagnosis, the code is the class" is a decision about *this* value, so a
+/// spec can assert it directly and a subprocess test asserts the same decision from outside. The
+/// binary that writes these bytes is a handful of lines because there is nothing left to decide.
+#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
+pub struct Outcome {
+    /// What the process would exit with.
+    code: u8,
+    /// What standard output would have received.
+    stdout: String,
+    /// What standard error would have received.
+    stderr: String,
+}
+
+impl Outcome {
+    /// The bytes standard output would have received.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn stdout(&self) -> &str {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// The bytes standard error would have received.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn stderr(&self) -> &str {
+        todo!()
+    }
+
+    /// The code the process would have ended with.
+    #[must_use]
+    pub fn code(&self) -> u8 {
+        todo!()
+    }
+}
