+//! The window arithmetic and the five aggregates: what a question about the store is made of, and
+//! nothing else.
+//!
+//! Step `lakelet/4.10-downsampling`. A store of records has no answers in it — an answer is what
+//! you get when you ask records a question, and the question every dashboard, alert and capacity
+//! plan asks is *"how did this move over time"*. That question is about windows, and this module
+//! is the whole of it: a [`Window`] is a width, a [`Range`] is two numbers, an [`Aggregate`] is
+//! one of five ways to summarize a window, and [`downsample`] is the pass that answers.
+//!
+//! # A window is a bucket of the time axis, not of the data
+//!
+//! The one decision everything else follows from is where a window's edges are. They are on the
+//! **absolute grid**: window `n` covers `[n * width, (n + 1) * width)`, counted from the Unix
+//! epoch, and [`Window::partition`] hands the answer to the same
+//! [`Partition`](crate::engine::retention::partition::Partition) arithmetic
+//! `lakelet/4.9-retention` snaps its cutoff with — the same `floor(ts / width) * width`, the same
+//! half-open `[start, end)`.
+//!
+//! The alternative — a window anchored at the first record the pass happens to see — looks
+//! harmless and is not:
+//!
+//! * **the same question would answer differently as the data moves.** One more sample at the
+//!   front shifts every boundary, so a dashboard refreshed twice in an hour draws two different
+//!   step functions for the same hour, and neither of them is wrong.
+//! * **two readers would disagree.** A retention pass counts in whole buckets
+//!   (`Policy::width_millis`), and a query's buckets that are not on the same grid cannot be
+//!   compared with what the store still keeps — "the last hour" would be one thing to the pass
+//!   and another to the query over it.
+//! * **a bucket would stop being a function of the time it covers.** Two servers querying the
+//!   same store from two different slices of it would print different `start` values, which is
+//!   the one thing a time series *is*.
+//!
+//! # A bucket counts what is in the range, not what is in the window
+//!
+//! A range begins and ends where the caller asked, and a window begins and ends on the grid, so
+//! the first and last windows of an answer are usually **partial**: they hold the records between
+//! the range's edge and the window's edge, and no others. There is no reaching outside the range
+//! to fill them — a bucket that borrowed the samples before `from_millis` would be an answer to a
+//! question nobody asked — and [`Bucket::count`] is the honest number beside [`Bucket::value`]:
+//! `12` next to `0.31` says the mean of twelve samples, `1` next to `0.31` says the mean of one,
+//! and a reader who cannot tell them apart is reading a number that means nothing.
+//!
+//! # An empty window is absent, not zero
+//!
+//! A window with no records in the range does not appear in [`Answer::buckets`] at all. It could
+//! be printed as `0` — and that is exactly the lie this rule refuses: a stored counter that
+//! really measured nothing and an hour in which nothing was *reported* are completely different
+//! facts (a store that is down and a store that is idle), and one number claiming to be both
+//! makes the difference unreadable for ever. The gap is information, and a caller who wants to
+//! draw it as a gap has what it needs: the windows the range touches ([`Window::partitions`])
+//! minus the buckets the answer holds.
+//!
+//! # `series`, and why the filter is an exact name
+//!
+//! `series` is an exact match on the record's series — no prefix, no pattern, no case folding —
+//! and `None` means *every* series. Both halves matter: a filter that matched loosely would
+//! silently fold one host's samples into another's, and `None` is what a caller uses when they
+//! genuinely want the fleet's traffic together. Mixing series in one `mean` is usually the bug
+//! behind a graph nobody can explain, which is why `lakelet/4.11-query-engine` makes the grammar
+//! ask for a series rather than defaulting to all of them.
+//!
+//! # The tolerance is a number in the code
+//!
+//! [`TOLERANCE`] and [`agree`] are where this step states how far a downsampled answer may be from
+//! the raw one. Floating point is not associative, and — far more important — **the mean of the
+//! buckets is not the mean of the range**: they agree exactly only when every window holds records
+//! with the same mean, and the difference otherwise is a property of how the range falls across
+//! the windows, not of the arithmetic. So every comparison of two computed numbers in this step's
+//! spec goes through [`agree`], and none of them writes `==` between two `f64`s.
+
+use crate::engine::retention::partition::Partition;
+use crate::protocol::batch::Record;
+
+/// How far apart two numbers may be and still be the same answer: a twentieth of the larger one.
+///
+/// This is a **declared** tolerance, not a floating-point epsilon, and the number is chosen from
+/// what it has to cover rather than from taste. The comparison it exists for is the mean of the
+/// buckets against the raw mean of the range, and those differ by however unevenly the range's
+/// records fall across its partial windows: a twentieth is wide enough that a window boundary
+/// landing a sample or two off the data's own edges does not change what an operator reads, and
+/// narrow enough that an answer computed from the *wrong records* — another series, a bucket that
+/// filled itself from outside the range — does not fit inside it. The spec carries both halves,
+/// and the budget lane measures the real gap this step's own workload produces.
+pub const TOLERANCE: f64 = 0.05;
+
+/// `true` when `left` and `right` are the same answer, inside [`TOLERANCE`].
+///
+/// The scale is the larger of the two magnitudes with a floor of one, and the floor is what makes
+/// "the same answer" mean the same thing at every size: a purely relative test would call two
+/// values a thousandth apart disagreeing when they are near zero, and `agree(0.0, 0.0)` — this
+/// step's most common comparison — has to be true. `NaN` agrees with nothing, itself included: a
+/// value that is not a number is not an answer this can certify, and quietly certifying it is how
+/// a broken aggregation reaches a dashboard looking healthy.
+#[must_use]
+pub fn agree(left: f64, right: f64) -> bool {
+    todo!("lakelet/4.10-downsampling: TOLERANCE is declared above; the comparison is yours")
+}
